<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Criollo people</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as creole</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Criollos, or creoles, were people of Spanish descent born in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> during the colonial period. They formed much of the colonial elite but were often excluded from the highest offices in favour of Spanish-born officials. Their resentment, sharpened by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bourbon-reforms">Bourbon Reforms</a>, fuelled the independence movements that ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire</a> in the Americas.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Society remained shaped by colonial hierarchy, with a <b>creole</b> elite of landowners and merchants above mestizo and pardo artisans and farmers, indigenous communities and enslaved Africans.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gran-colombia">Gran Colombia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>…a governing junta formed on 18 September 1810, a date still celebrated as the national holiday, opening the period known as the Patria Vieja, in which <b>creole</b> leaders such as José Miguel Carrera and Bernardo O'Higgins quarrelled while Viceroy José Fernando de Abascal sent royalist forces from Peru.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-chile">Republic of Chile · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Quito’s <b>creole</b> elite formed one of the earliest Spanish American juntas on 10 August 1809, but royalist forces suppressed it, and independence came only with the campaign of Antonio José de Sucre, whose victory at the Battle of Pichincha on 24 May 1822 freed Quito.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-ecuador">Republic of Ecuador · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In colonial times Honduras was a thinly settled province whose silver mines around Tegucigalpa and cattle ranches supported a small <b>creole</b> elite, while its governor and bishop resided at Comayagua.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-honduras">Republic of Honduras · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The state’s territory was also incomplete: the Caribbean Mosquito Coast, home to Miskitu and <b>Creole</b> communities, lay under a British protectorate, and in 1848 British forces seized the port of San Juan del Norte, which they renamed Greytown.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-nicaragua">Republic of Nicaragua · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Lima was the seat of Spain’s oldest and richest South American viceroyalty, heir to the core of the Inca Empire, and its <b>creole</b> elite, shaken by the great rebellion of Túpac Amaru II in 1780–1781, stayed mostly loyal to the crown.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-peru">Republic of Peru · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
